      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral$\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={e}^{2x}-2x.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}-2=2({e}^{2x}-1)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=({e}^{2x}-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}-2x|}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/id": "0d2baee3-b19d-5250-8ec4-8b4529ea71a4",
